# Token-Optimized Cursor Rules for Databricks Medallion Data Pipelines

# Core Principles
- Always generate clean, modular PySpark & SQL code targetting Databricks Runtime (Delta Lake).
- Keep code concise, explicit, and strongly typed. Avoid redundant docstrings or excessive boilerplate to optimize token usage.
- Prefer PySpark DataFrame API for Bronze/Silver and Spark SQL for Gold aggregations.

# Databricks & Delta Lake Standards
- Bronze: Raw ingestion into Delta. Always append ingestion metadata (`_ingested_at`, `_source_file`).
- Silver: Append `quality_check_result` (STRUCT/JSON) and `is_valid` (BOOLEAN). Never drop raw records in Silver.
- Gold: Query only `is_valid = True` records from Silver. Materialize as Delta tables.
- Use `dbutils.widgets` or standard `spark.sql` parameters for configurable paths.

# Code & Style Guidelines
- PySpark: Use `F.col`, `F.when`, `F.coalesce`, `F.to_date`, `F.count`, `F.sum`.
- Standard column naming: snake_case (`customer_id`, `order_status`, `total_amount`).
- Include brief inline comments explaining non-trivial business logic or DQ rules.
- Testability: Keep data transformations in pure functions `transform_df(df)` so they can be unit-tested with PyTest.
